You just described a simmer trajectory, so I'd say that simmer is
indeed appropriate.

> This seems very straightforward until I considered the inter-relatedness of
> these three trajectories.  For instance, a bag of typeA and a bag of typeB
> may arrive simultaneously from the same purchase, and I only need one store
> employee to move them to their storage areas (one bag each hand!).  However,
> these two bags may then be retreived from the storage area at the same time
> (requiring one seizure of a store employee), or at separate times, requiring
> two separate seizures of store employees (or two seizures of the same
> employee).
>
> How might I go about including the following considerations in the
> trajectories:
>
> 1) Correlation between arrivals - I can identify an empirical probability
> that each purchase will contain one, two, or all three bag types.  How can I
> generate arrivals along trajectories that draw from a common empirical
> probability like this?
>
> 2) Correlation of the time spent in storage areas - sometimes, two bags from
> the same purchase will be removed from their respective storage areas
> simultaneously, sometimes not.  Can I write the storage area dwell time
> arguments for two bags, from the same purchase, so that sometimes the bags
> have the same dwell time, and sometimes they don't?
>
> 3) When moving multiple bags from the counter to the storage area, how do I
> only seize one employee even though they are on different bagtype-specific
> trajectories? Similarly, how do I seize an employee once if two bags of the
> same purchase are retrieved at the same time, but twice if they are
> retrieved at different times?

Taking this into consideration, there is only one detail you must add
to your description above: an employee does not carry bags; they carry
*batches* of bags. So you only need to enclose your "carrying process"
between batch and separate. A simmer batch is quite versatile. It
allows you not only to define the maximum number of bags that an
employee is able to carry, but also a timeout, which controls the
correlation time (i.e., how long the lag can be in order to consider
two bags as "simultaneous").

Regards,
Iñaki

