You cannot do that with a batch activity, because the batch size is a
constant. You need to generate 1, 2, 3 or 4 bags at the same time. So
instead of using 3 independent generators, use 1 generator as the
following:

bag_generator <- function() rep(rexp(1), sample(1:4, size=1,
prob=c(.6, .2, .1, .1)))

Then, you'll need to distribute these bags to your trajectories, i.e.,
decide the type for each bag. You can use a branch:

distributor <- trajectory() %>%
  branch(
    some_function_here, # must return 1, 2 or 3 with some probability
    continue=rep(TRUE, 3),
    AmbientBag_trajectory,
    RefrigBag_trajectory,
    FrozenBag_trajectory
  )

And your generator feeds this trajectory.

> 2) they move as a batch through the front cashier, being moved by the
> backroom worker to the storage areas

Add batch() and separate() where appropriate in your trajectories.

> 3) then they're unbatched and move to their respective storage areas.  Bag
> storage times can be assumed to be gamma-distributed.  If possible, I want
> to have the option that either:
>
> 3a) bag storage times within the same order are all identical - meaning they
> are placed in their respective temperature storage areas, but are retrieved
> simultaneously from a single sampling of the gamma distribution.  They then
> seize the same backroom worker and exit the system.

Set an attribute to the batch, before the separate activity, to set an
identical time for the batch. Then set the timeout to use that
attribute.

> 3b) bag storage times within the same order are unique, and are retrieved
> separtately from three samples of the gamma distribution.  They then seize
> the backroom worker separately and exit the system.

Don't do the latter.

> 3c) for two bags from different initial batches, even if their expiration
> time in storage is nearly simultaneous, they need to seize separate backroom
> workers.

Don't name the batch, so that they are exclusive for each trajectory.

>   set_attribute("trajectory_start_time", function() now(.env=store_sim_env))

You don't need to do this. simmer automatically monitors everything,
and you can get a data frame with everything that happened during the
simulation. So you can simply drop all this set_attribute() stuff.

> #generators for different bag types, poisson process with rate calculated by
> average number of bags expected to see in a day.
>   simmer::add_generator("ambientbag", AmbientBag_trajectory, function()
> rpois(1,(minutes_operation/(daily_bag_forecast*pct_bags_ambient))),mon=2)
> %>%
>   simmer::add_generator("refrigbag", RefrigBag_trajectory, function()
> rpois(1,(minutes_operation/(daily_bag_forecast*pct_bags_refrig))),mon=2) %>%
>   simmer::add_generator("frozenbag", FrozenBag_trajectory, function()
> rpois(1,(minutes_operation/(daily_bag_forecast*pct_bags_frozen))),mon=2)

That's not a Poisson process. This is a Poisson distribution, which is
very different. The right function here is rexp, not rpois. Anyway,
I'd use the generator I've provided in my first comment.

Iñaki

