>>>
>>> Up to now, when I needed a warmup period I only took this into account in
>>> the analysis of the results (and potentially in the runtime).
>>> If w is the warmup time, I simply filtered the monitor's results to
>>> exclude everything before w. A nice to have could e.g. be the specification
>>> of a warmup time in `run()` which is then taken into account by the
>>> `get_mon_` functions.
>>
>>
>> I think simmer should *not* filter data for two reasons:
>>
>> If the transitory is complex and needs to be determined statistically, you
>> need to do it by yourself in the analysis phase.
>> If the transitory is straightforward, you can simply filter everything
>> before t=t_0, and that's it: there is no gain in simmer doing so.
>>
>> It might be some gain if the data saturates your RAM. But if that's the
>> case, the warm-up is probably the smallest of your problems. :D
>>
>> But turning to the pre-initialisation strategy, there may be systems too
>> dependent on the initial conditions (for instance, reaching very different
>> stationary regimes depending on the initial state), so that you may need to
>> set up these initial conditions maching your particular problem.
>>
>> I would like to spend time on this issue. At some point... I promise. :D
>> For instance, it would be great if we could identify a simple problem, a
>> specific simulation, exhibiting the behaviour I've described.
>>
>> --
